WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:02.302
This back and forth between

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.270
Trump and DeSantis

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.873
keeps going on during today

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.141
both of their stops.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.776
They're going back and forth

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.778
attacking each other. Tell us about that.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.146
Yeah, and I should note, based

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:14.647
on what Andrew just reported there,

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:15.715
just a few moments ago,

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:16.082
the Trump

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.718
campaign sent out a link to his article,

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:19.586
basically flagging

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:20.220
those comments

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:20.854
that he found

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:21.988
that DeSantis made

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:23.289
about Foushee in the past.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.491
But it is in Iowa,

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:25.625
which is the latest stage

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.494
where they have been treating

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.162
these threats or these insults.

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:30.263
I should note to one another,

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:30.897
you're really saying

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.499
DeSantis, who for months

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.134
kind of absorbed a lot of the insults

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:35.502
coming his way from the former

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.169
president,

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:37.604
is now counterpunching,

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:39.372
using one of Trump's favorite words.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:40.974
Trump himself is in Des Moines today.

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:43.143
Jake, he also was going after DeSantis

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:44.144
several times,

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:45.211
talking about the disparity

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:45.945
in their polling,

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.548
how far ahead he is of Governor DeSantis.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:49.582
When you look at the average

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.618
of those polls right now nationwide,

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:52.752
but also talking about Iowa,

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.788
specifically with the former president

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.823
saying that he believes there's no way

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.224
that they could lose Iowa.

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:58.591
Obviously,

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.026
it would be incredibly devastating

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:00.860
if he did it,

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:01.261
given he's

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:02.162
the former

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.763
former sitting over for president

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.532
and the Republican frontrunner

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.499
at this time.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:08.268
He also, Jake, went after DeSantis

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.002
for those comments

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:10.303
he made recently,

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:11.538
basically arguing

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.173
that if Trump was reelected,

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.442
he'd only have one more term in office

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:16.543
while DeSantis would be able

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:17.010
to potentially

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.645
have eight years in office.

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:19.879
And the difference that he could make

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.714
with Republican priorities on that.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.116
Trump pushed back on that today

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:26.653
but when I heard

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.155
DeSantis go out and say

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:30.824
and talk about eight years,

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:31.891
we need eight years.

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:32.859
You don't need eight years,

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:33.760
you need six months,

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.062
we can turn this thing around so quickly

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.364
if you need eight years.

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.400
Who the hell wants to wait eight years?

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.135
You don't need eight years.

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:42.769
President Trump

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:43.970
doesn't do it in six months.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.004
Here you go now, right?

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:46.473
You don't want to hear me now.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:49.809
Why didn't he do it his first four years?

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.377
No, it's clear.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:52.612
This is what this is going to look like.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:53.413
Obviously, DeSantis

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:54.447
has to walk a fine line

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:55.215
when it comes to this

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.250
because he is trying to get Trump voters

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:57.951
to vote for him

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:59.052
and to come into his circle,

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:01.154
but also you're really seeing him go

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:01.855
after Trump

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:03.656
in a way that we had not seen before

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.425
his official campaign launch last week.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:07.560
Hmm.

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:08.628
And Caitlin, you were

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:09.662
part of the terrific

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.030
reporting team, along with Paul

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.165
Ryan and Caitlin Plants

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:12.699
that broke

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.067
that story about special counsel

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:16.469
Jack Smith obtaining a recording of Trump

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:17.604
discussing how

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.140
he had a classified document

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.742
about an attack on Iran by the U.S.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:23.643
that he took with him

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:24.777
from the White House.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.013
Tell us about Trump's response today.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:29.015
He's not really responding much at all

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.251
or Jeff Zeleny tried to ask him about it.

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:33.553
He did not answer the shouted questions

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:35.722
from CNN in response to this reporting.

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:36.723
Did it engage?

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:37.157
We of course,

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.691
we saw his attorney, Jim Trusty,

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.560
last night talking about it publicly,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.494
but not really offering

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.396
a lot of clarity about this document,

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:45.198
about whether or not it's been returned

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.266
to the National Archives

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.734
or why it was taken to Bedminster

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:48.735
in the first place.

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.436
Trump did comment to the Cedar

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.471
Rapids Gazette.

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.306
He called the reporting fake news.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.073
Obviously, it's

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.275
not just been confirmed

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.209
and written by CNN,

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.444
but also confirmed by other outlets.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:02:59.679
But you didn't see Trump

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.080
responding to it on camera.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.582
He did make a comment today

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.349
when he was speaking

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.385
at that similar event that you referenced

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:06.953
to the investigations into him.

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:08.821
He said he is, quote, the victim

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:09.722
of many things.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:10.990
Obviously, that's how he views it.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:11.858
The question,

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:12.892
though, is whether or not

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.694
he has greater legal exposure

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:15.895
following that reporting

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:16.863
and what we now know,

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.064
the special counsel has

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:19.098
in their possession

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:22.936
All right,
Kaitlan Collins, thanks so much.

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:23.970
Let's discuss

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.339
with my Augusta panel, Abby Phillip.

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.475
Trump is known for using derogatory

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.244
nicknames for his political adversaries.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.178
He's created

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:35.114
a litany of them of Ron DeSantis today.

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.583
The Florida governor responded

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:38.952
That's part of why voters

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.354
did not return him to the White House.

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:42.488
Take a listen.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:45.058
I think that's one of the reasons he's

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.226
not in the White House now,

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:47.627
because I think he alienated

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:49.395
too many voters

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.364
for things that really don't matter.

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:53.299
So I don't care what they say about me

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:54.534
in terms of name calling.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.301
Although, you know,

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:55.902
I would say

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.237
he needs to call me a winner

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:58.571
because we've won in Florida

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.540
over and over again.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.976
Have voters actually soured on Trump over

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:03.576
the name calling?

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.477
Is there evidence of that?

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:05.545
I think that

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.080
what DeSantis is referring to

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.448
is a different set of voters.

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:10.149
I think the general election

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.551
voters, the independents,

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:12.452
the persuadables,

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.720
the people that

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.655
a Republican nominee would need to win

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:17.290
in order to win the presidency.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.691
There is evidence

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.293
not just in the presidential election

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:20.994
in 2020,

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:22.862
but also in the midterms in

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.931
20, 18 and 20, 20,

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.033
the congressional races in 20, 20,

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.636
and then also even in 20, 22 that voters

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:31.537
are kind

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:34.741
of souring on the divisiveness.

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:36.976
They think both sides are pretty toxic.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:37.810
But in particular,

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.679
I think Trump's toxic ness

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:40.980
was damaging to him.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:41.814
But when it comes to

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.383
the Republican primary,

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:45.351
that's a different set of voters

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.887
that that DeSantis has to contend with.

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:49.789
And this is where it gets tough for him

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.891
because Republican primary voters

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:52.525
they kind of

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.427
do like Trump's,

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:54.861
you know,

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:57.630
kind of fighter instincts the way that he

00:04:58.231 --> 00:04:59.565
is sort of entertaining

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:01.200
and how he makes nicknames

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.669
out of his opponents.

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:03.503
And DeSantis

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:04.771
is going to have to figure out,

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:06.406
how do I deal with that?

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:08.574
He's trying a logic argument

00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:09.676
We'll see if that works.

00:05:09.676 --> 00:05:11.911
But I think folks need to come to terms

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:12.412
with the fact

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:14.080
that Trump's entertainment

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.548
factor is a huge part of his

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.417
appeal to Republican voters.

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:21.487
And former Congresswoman Mia Love.

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.223
Donald Trump campaigning

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.825
stood by

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:26.859
the COVID vaccine,

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:28.528
said a lot of people out there

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:29.629
appreciate it.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.097
He stood by

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:32.799
even though one voter took them

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:35.134
on, saying that they didn't like the jab.

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:35.868
DeSantis

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.337
is aligning himself

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.106
with the people who are anti-vaccine.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.175
What do you make of that?

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:45.244
I think DeSantis is God.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:46.446
He's making some choices.

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:48.915
Some good. Fight back was good.

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.784
Some to fight against, to fight against

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:53.619
or attack

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.521
Donald Trump on criminal justice reform.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:56.889
Those aren't good.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.459
Obviously, there are people who

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:01.260
we are looking.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.596
We see with the COVID vaccine has done

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:04.931
and how many lives it saved.

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:05.965
That's not

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.834
that's not a good thing

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.902
for him to fight him on.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:12.505
I mean, he's making some choices

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:13.506
and some of those choices

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:14.974
aren't going to work

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:16.609
with mainstream Republicans.

00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:17.543
And he's got to get

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:18.978
the mainstream Republicans

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:21.347
care.

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:21.948
And more and more

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.249
Republicans are teasing

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:24.751
possible presidential runs,

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:27.153
including Mike Pence next week.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.155
And I want to get your reaction

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:29.789
to what New Hampshire

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:31.457
Governor Chris Sununu

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.426
who is also considering running.

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.461
He says about that

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:37.597
come November, December

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:38.998
of this year, if you're not polling well,

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:40.700
get your butt out of this race.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.534
Let's narrow it down

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:42.635
to a couple of candidates.

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:44.470
That's more fair to the process.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:44.771
And I think

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:45.738
that's going to be the discipline

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:47.006
not just for the candidates,

00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:48.408
but for the donor base.

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:49.876
They have to tell their candidates

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:50.510
to get out, right.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:51.811
They and I think they will.

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:52.278
And this is not

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:56.048
going to be a repeat of 2016

00:06:56.182 --> 00:06:57.083
so

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.786
that he said something similar

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:02.889
to me, Karen, on Sunday.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:04.023
What do you make of that?

00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:05.625
The idea of like by December,

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:07.393
you need to start clearing the field?

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:08.194
Well, no.

00:07:08.194 --> 00:07:09.896
One, I think very few

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:10.797
will actually do that

00:07:10.797 --> 00:07:11.864
because the whole point is

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:13.699
you want to get to Iowa,

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:14.934
see how you do

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.502
and see if you can get some momentum.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:17.503
And given that,

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:18.805
you know,
in a number of instances,

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:19.539
the person who wins

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:20.840
Iowa is not necessarily

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.542
the person who ends up being the nominee.

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:24.811
So I doubt that many will

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.112
you know, take that advice.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.747
But clearly, the concern he's

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:29.081
trying to put forward here

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:29.816
is that

00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:31.517
the more people who get into the race,

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:34.987
the more fractured the vote becomes.

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:37.256
Which could create more of an opportunity

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:38.324
for Donald Trump,

00:07:38.324 --> 00:07:39.091
which is,

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:39.592
you know, as

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:40.726
you know, one of the reasons

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.295
that people like

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:43.329
former Governor Chris

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:44.430
Christie is thinking about

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:45.932
getting in with the idea

00:07:45.932 --> 00:07:48.301
of someone needs to take Donald Trump

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:49.735
on more directly.

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:52.038
And as our own reporting indicated,

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:53.606
not just off

00:07:53.606 --> 00:07:55.107
camera or off the stage,

00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:56.075
but on this stage

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:57.777
more directly challenge him.

00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:00.346
So I don't think many of the candidates

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:02.582
or potential candidates will heed

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:04.183
that message from Sununu.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:05.318
But it was a nice try.

